<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Category Theory on A Moment's Rest</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/tags/category-theory/</link><description>Recent content in Category Theory on A Moment's Rest</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 10 Jul 2026 15:09:16 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://a-moment096.github.io/en/tags/category-theory/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>The Product and Coproduct of Vector Spaces</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/product_and_coproduct_of_vector_space/</link><pubDate>Sun, 05 Oct 2025 19:55:20 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/product_and_coproduct_of_vector_space/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Eyjafjalla.jpg" alt="Featured image of post The Product and Coproduct of Vector Spaces" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;What exactly is the difference between the direct product (product) and direct sum (coproduct) of vector spaces? Where does their distinction originate? This question that troubled me for a long time has finally found its answer today — let&amp;rsquo;s take a look together~&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The selected song is from the album &lt;a class="link" href="https://music.163.com/#/album?id=72378630" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;two&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a class="link" href="https://space.bilibili.com/316381099" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;鹿乃&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, which I recently asked a friend to buy from Japan (hasn&amp;rsquo;t arrived yet). It is also a song by my beloved producer &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.nicovideo.jp/user/865371" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;&lt;strong&gt;電ポルP&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (Koyori, also called Utility Pole P, because the song illustrations often feature utility poles), released in 2014 and sung by v flower: &lt;strong&gt;曖昧劣情Lover&lt;/strong&gt;. It&amp;rsquo;s truly wonderful — I really like it. The featured image is an AI-generated picture of the adorable Eyjafjalla, produced by a certain mysterious group-chat friend&amp;rsquo;s friend &lt;strong&gt;派佬&lt;/strong&gt;. I hope you enjoy it~&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>What Exactly Is a Function!?</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/concept_of_function/</link><pubDate>Tue, 23 Sep 2025 17:41:51 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/concept_of_function/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/%E7%AC%94%E4%B8%AD%E7%9A%84%E5%91%93%E5%96%83%E4%B9%8B%E5%A2%83.jpg" alt="Featured image of post What Exactly Is a Function!?" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;We frequently encounter the so-called &lt;strong&gt;function&lt;/strong&gt;, in mathematics, physics, and even in programs — we see its figure everywhere. Yet, what exactly is it? You may have an answer in your own heart. Here I&amp;rsquo;ll venture to compare definitions of functions across various textbooks and chat about this concept that we may be very familiar with.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The featured image is still selected from &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.pixiv.net/en/users/8605991" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;fasnakegod&lt;/a&gt;&amp;rsquo;s &lt;a class="link" href="https://www.pixiv.net/en/artworks/115191853" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 &gt;笔中的呓喃之境&lt;/a&gt;. It&amp;rsquo;s truly beautiful, and Aya is so cute. The song is selected from a classic fingerstyle guitar piece: Masaaki Kishibe&amp;rsquo;s &amp;ldquo;Time Travel&amp;rdquo; — light and pleasant. I like it very much (even tried learning it) (didn&amp;rsquo;t succeed).&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Year of the Snake, Snake Lemma!</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/snake_lemma/</link><pubDate>Thu, 27 Feb 2025 00:00:00 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/math_note/snake_lemma/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Post%20Shelter-Inaba%20Kumori.png" alt="Featured image of post Year of the Snake, Snake Lemma!" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I&amp;rsquo;ve watched so many videos proving the Snake Lemma, so I figured I&amp;rsquo;d give it a shot too~ It&amp;rsquo;s the Year of the Snake, participation matters, right&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The featured image is by &lt;a class="link" href="https://twitter.com/NKNK_NGRMS" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
